# Line endings, decided in the repository rather than per machine.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: this project is edited on Windows and built on
# ubuntu-latest, and it ships shell scripts that a CRLF byte breaks — `bash` reads
# the carriage return as part of the last argument. Without a .gitattributes the
# answer depends on each clone's core.autocrlf, which is a setting nobody reviews
# and every machine gets to disagree about. REFACTOR-STANDARDS already records the
# cost of the related class of bug: "a check that passes for the wrong reason
# (wrong directory, line-ending-blind comparison, a probe that never ran) is
# discarded, not read as green."
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises to LF in the repository AND checks out LF in the
# working tree, on every platform. It overrides core.autocrlf, which is the point:
# the file wins over the machine.
#
# ON A WINDOWS CHECKOUT, and this is worth knowing before it surprises someone:
# a clone with core.autocrlf=true has CRLF on disk today. `eol=lf` makes git want
# LF there instead, so the first `git status` after this merges may list files as
# modified. That is a working-tree refresh, not a content change — the repository
# already stores LF for all of them, which the pull request that added this file
# proved with `git add --renormalize .`. Check the local setting with
#   git config --get core.autocrlf
# and prefer `false` or `input`; either leaves this file in charge.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit for the files where a CRLF is not a cosmetic problem but a broken
# script or a broken heredoc.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Binary. Only the types this repository actually tracks are listed — a
# .gitattributes naming formats that are not here is a claim about content that
# does not exist. Measured with `git ls-files "*.<ext>"`: 21 png, 1 ico, and no
# jpg, woff, woff2, ttf, jar or pdf anywhere.
*.png -text
*.ico -text
